# Target C — mixed-wall repacking for a hull-augmentation outer pocket

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Status:** proof-only research candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.

This theorem applies to the convex **outer pockets inside the force hull** from canonical
Sections 67 and 77. It does not apply to the distinct exterior caps between the force
hull and the full source hull in Sections 77.2/80/162.

A Section-67 outer pocket has boundary consisting of

- one added force-hull side `[u,v]`, which is not assumed to be a unit contact; and
- one complementary old outer-contact path made of unit contact edges.

The full one-unit geometric collar is unnecessary on the contact-path side. The added
side still needs ordinary distance-one erosion, while the contact path is protected by
its original boundary source vertices and the unit-edge crossing inequality.

---

## 1. Canonical pocket geometry

Let `A` be the triangle-free global diameter minimizer, let `K=conv(A)`, and let
`F` be the force hull. Let `C subset F` be one convex outer pocket from the
hull-augmentation construction in canonical Section 67.3.

Write

\[
 \partial C=[u,v]\cup W,
\tag{1.1}
\]

where `[u,v]` is the one added force-hull side and

\[
 W=(u=p_0,p_1,\ldots,p_\ell=v)
\tag{1.2}
\]

is the complementary old contact path. Every old path edge is a unit contact:

\[
 |p_{i+1}-p_i|=1.
\tag{1.3}
\]

Let `Z_C` be the contact-isolated source points assigned to this pocket by canonical
Section 67.6, including the convention that a contact-isolated source point lying on
the added side is assigned to the unique adjacent interior pocket. Put `z_C=|Z_C|`.

All assigned points lie outside the positive contact core. Every actual diameter
endpoint is a source-hull extreme point and belongs to the loaded force-hull vertex set;
in particular an assigned contact-isolated point on the relative interior of the added
side is not a diameter endpoint.

---

## 2. Mixed-wall safe region

Define

\[
 S_{mix}(C):=\left\{
 y\in\operatorname{int}C:
 \operatorname{dist}(y,[u,v])\ge1,
 \quad |y-p_i|\ge1\ \text{for }0\le i\le\ell
 \right\}.
\tag{2.1}
\]

Compared with the full inner parallel body, this keeps the unit collar only on the
artificial side `[u,v]` and replaces contact-path erosion by discrete guards at the
boundary source vertices.

---

## 3. Mixed-wall shield

We use the elementary crossing fact: if a segment of length `<1` properly crosses a
unit edge `[p,q]`, and its outside endpoint is at distance at least one from `p,q`,
then its inside endpoint is at distance `<1` from both `p,q`.

### Theorem 3.1 — pocket mixed-wall shield

If `y in S_mix(C)` and `x` is any old source point outside `int C`, then

\[
 \boxed{|x-y|\ge1.}
\tag{3.1}
\]

#### Proof

Suppose `|x-y|<1`. Follow the segment from `y` toward `x` and let `r` be its first
intersection with `partial C`.

If `r` lies in the relative interior of the added side `[u,v]`, then

\[
 \operatorname{dist}(y,[u,v])\le |y-r|<|x-y|<1,
\]

contradicting (2.1).

If `r=p_i` is a boundary source vertex, then either `x=p_i`, in which case (2.1)
already gives `|x-y|>=1`, or source unit separation gives `|x-p_i|>=1`. In the latter
case `p_i` lies strictly between `x,y`, so

\[
 |x-y|=|x-p_i|+|p_i-y|\ge2,
\]

a contradiction.

Otherwise `r` lies in the relative interior of one old unit-contact edge
`[p_i,p_{i+1}]`. If `x` is one of its endpoints the vertex guard already applies.
Otherwise source unit separation gives

\[
 |x-p_i|,|x-p_{i+1}|\ge1.
\]

Convexity makes the crossing proper, so the unit-edge crossing lemma yields

\[
 |y-p_i|<1,
 \qquad |y-p_{i+1}|<1,
\]

contradicting (2.1). These cases exhaust `partial C`. `square`

A source point on the relative interior of the added side is protected directly by
the distance-to-side condition; no contact-edge claim is made for the added side.

---

## 4. Diameter-cover repacking

Let

\[
 M_{mix}(C):=\sup\{|Y|:Y\subset S_{mix}(C),\ |y-y'|\ge1\}.
\tag{4.1}
\]

Let `H` be the complete actual diameter graph and let `U` be a vertex cover of `H`.
If

\[
 M_{mix}(C)\ge z_C+|U|,
\tag{4.2}
\]

choose a unit-separated `Y subset S_mix(C)` of that size and define

\[
 A':=(A\setminus(Z_C\cup U))\cup Y.
\tag{4.3}
\]

### Theorem 4.1 — mixed-wall cover repacking

Under (4.2), `A'` is a same-cardinality unit-separated source with

\[
 \boxed{\operatorname{diam}A'<D.}
\tag{4.4}
\]

#### Proof

Assigned chamber points are not actual diameter endpoints, so `Z_C cap U` is empty.
Cardinality is preserved.

Old-old unit separation is unchanged, new-new separation holds by construction, and
Theorem 3.1 gives new-old separation because every retained old source point lies
outside `int C`.

The cover `U` meets every actual diameter edge, so no retained old-old pair remains at
distance `D`. Every new point lies in `int C subset int F subset int K`; every distance
from an interior point of `K` to a point of `K` is strictly below `D`. Thus every pair
in the finite set `A'` has distance `<D` except possibly retained old-old pairs, which
were already handled by the cover. Hence `diam A'<D`, contradicting global minimality.
`square`

The replacement need not be triangle-free.

---

## 5. Lattice capacity and the exact residual

Let

\[
 s=|u-v|,
\]

so `per(C)=s+ell`. Let `k_C=ell+1` be the augmented graph-boundary vertex count: one
added side plus `ell` old contact edges form a simple polygon with `ell+1` vertices.
Define

\[
 \sigma_C={A_C\over a_0}+{s+\ell\over2}+1-z_C,
 \qquad
 r_C:=\sigma_C-k_C,
 \qquad a_0={\sqrt3\over2}.
\tag{5.1}
\]

The canonical pocket Oler inequality is exactly `r_C>=0`.

Let

\[
 c_*={\pi\over6}+{\sqrt3\over4},
 \qquad
 h_*={\pi\over3}-{\sqrt3\over4},
 \qquad
 \kappa_*={\pi\over3\sqrt3},
 \qquad
 \lambda_*={2\over\sqrt3}+{1\over2}.
\tag{5.2}
\]

The companion contact-chain area theorem proves

\[
 \operatorname{area}S_{mix}(C)
 \ge A_C-s-\ell c_*-h_*.
\tag{5.3}
\]

Translation averaging of the unit triangular lattice gives

\[
 M_{mix}(C)
 \ge\left\lfloor{\operatorname{area}S_{mix}(C)\over a_0}\right\rfloor.
\tag{5.4}
\]

Since

\[
 {A_C\over a_0}=z_C+r_C+{\ell\over2}-{s\over2},
\]

we obtain the explicit realized-spare bound

\[
 \boxed{
 M_{mix}(C)-z_C
 \ge
 \left\lfloor
 r_C-\kappa_*\ell-\lambda_*s-{h_*\over a_0}
 \right\rfloor.
 }
\tag{5.5}
\]

Consequently this pocket can absorb a minimum diameter cover whenever

\[
 r_C\ge
 \tau(H)+\kappa_*\ell+\lambda_*s+{h_*\over a_0}.
\tag{5.6}
\]

This is a constructive lower bound; no Oler inequality is reversed.

---

## 6. Scope boundary

This theorem concerns the Section-67/77 outer pockets which tile the old outer
positive-contact face **inside `F`**. It does not apply to the convex exterior caps in
`K\setminus int F` used in Sections 77.2, 80 and 162. For those exterior caps the old
outer boundary follows the source hull rather than a certified unit-contact path, so
the audited-working full-collar theorem of Section 165 remains the valid general
repacking mechanism.

No source point is deleted without replacement, and contact-isolated points are not
placed under the Section-57 affine-kernel theorem.
